  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday night through Friday)\nIssued at 133 AM MDT Sat Aug 29 2026\n\nThe upper high continues to shift eastward on Monday and while the\nridge axis will still have a grip on NM, it will be weaker\noverall. Models are indicating another vort max will shift\nnorthward across AZ Sunday night and move into NW NM early Monday. \nHowever, a few models suggest the vort max will be delayed a bit \nlater, and enter western NM Monday afternoon. If it arrives later,\nthe extra lift provided from this feature would allow for a few \nstronger storms with higher rain rates. After rainfall the \nprevious two days and wetter soils, high rainfall rates could lead\nto flooding. However, one big mitigating factor will be the storm\nmotions, which will be even higher than on Sunday. Storm motion \nwill be toward the north or northeast around 15 to 25 mph. If the\nvort max arrives in the morning, the early morning precipitation \nand cloud cover would limit destabilization and result in fewer \nand weaker storms overall.\n\nOn Tuesday, much drier air looks to push into western NM which will\nweaken and disrupt the moisture plume. Enough moisture will \nremain in place to recycle in the form of showers and\nthunderstorms over most high terrain areas but PWATs around\n0.6-0.8\" will limit any flooding potential. It also continues to \nlook like a new upper high will develop near the NM/MX border or \nthe upper high to the east will elongate back westward by \nWednesday and H5 heights will continue to rise through Friday. \nIsolated to scattered showers and thunderstorms will develop each \nafternoon over the high terrain, but overall will be a meager crop\nof convection. Temperatures will trend back upward across western\nNM and stay around 10 degrees above normal across eastern NM.",
